{
  format: 1
  name: module-value-install-move-lands-in-its-own-space
  suite: scenario
  spec: ["#modules", "§13.16", "§13.2", "§13.9"]
  note: '''
    §13.16 "Install / override": "Moving a module value into a slot installs it
    … `.modules[@id] <- unpack(@package)`". The slot is written relative to a row
    (`.companies[@company].modules[@name]`), while an instance is MOUNTED at the
    space's display path (`/companies/acme/modules`), which interleaves the
    containing-row key. That interleaving is the whole risk of the operator: get
    it wrong and the module installs into a sibling space while the call still
    reports success — a module state that is wrong and looks correct.

    So this is written as a DISCRIMINATION, not a smoke test. Two company rows
    each own a `$modules` space of the same declaration path, and the SAME root
    mutation installs into each by key, from two different packages seeded 5 and
    9. §13.2 fixes the expected answer independently of the implementation:
    "Installing the same package in each space creates two independent
    instances". So Acme's space must read 5 and Globex's 9.

    A lowering that dropped the containing-row key would mount both at
    `/modules`; one that used a fixed key would mount both under one company. In
    either case the two installs collide on ONE slot, §13.16's override drops the
    first, and both reads answer 9 — which is exactly what the second assertion
    catches. A run where only the first read is checked would pass.

    The read-back is the §13.9 interface aggregation over each space — the same
    boundary any parent uses — so what is asserted is that each instance is
    reachable THROUGH the space it was installed into, not merely that some
    install happened. The `entries` read closes §13.16 "Provenance and
    atomicity": each transition also carried the root's own `log` row, and both
    rows are there.

    The installs run as host/root-scope transitions (§13.10 lends the lifecycle
    privilege by scope; `red/lifecycle-operator-needs-host-privilege` is the
    matching refusal for a `$public` caller).
  '''

  packages: {
    host: {
      $liasse: 1
      $app: "t.modinstall.host@1.0.0"
      $model: {
        log: { $key: "id", id: "text" }
        companies: {
          $key: "id"
          id: "text"
          modules: {
            $modules: {
              $interfaces: {
                items: {
                  $view: { $key: "id", id: "text", qty: "int" }
                }
              }
            }
          }
        }
        $mut: {
          // §13.16: the move IS the install. The destination names the space by
          // its containing row; the source is the unpacked package.
          "provision({ id: text, company: text, name: text, package: blob })": [
            "e = .log + { id: @id }"
            ".companies[@company].modules[@name] <- unpack(@package)"
            "return e { id }"
          ]
        }
        $public: {
          entries: { $view: ".log { id }" }
          acme_items: { $view: '/companies["acme"].modules["sales"]::items' }
          globex_items: { $view: '/companies["globex"].modules["sales"]::items' }
        }
      }
      $data: { companies: { acme: {}, globex: {} } }
    }
    sales_five: {
      $liasse: 1
      $module: "t.modinstall.sales@1.0.0"
      $model: {
        items: { $key: "id", id: "text", qty: "int" }
      }
      $data: { items: { a: { qty: "5" } } }
      $expose: { items: { $view: ".items { id, qty }" } }
    }
    sales_nine: {
      $liasse: 1
      $module: "t.modinstall.sales.other@1.0.0"
      $model: {
        items: { $key: "id", id: "text", qty: "int" }
      }
      $data: { items: { a: { qty: "9" } } }
      $expose: { items: { $view: ".items { id, qty }" } }
    }
  }
  root: host

  steps: [
    // Two moves, into two spaces of the SAME declaration path under different rows.
    { module_lifecycle_call: { mutation: "provision", package: "sales_five"
        args: { id: "first", company: "acme", name: "sales" } }
      expect: { outcome: ok, value: { id: "first" } } }
    { module_lifecycle_call: { mutation: "provision", package: "sales_nine"
        args: { id: "second", company: "globex", name: "sales" } }
      expect: { outcome: ok, value: { id: "second" } } }

    // Each instance reads back through the space it was installed into, carrying
    // its own package's seed — two independent instances (§13.2), not one.
    { watch: "public.acme_items", id: "wa", expect_init: { value: [ { id: "a", qty: "5" } ] } }
    { watch: "public.globex_items", id: "wg", expect_init: { value: [ { id: "a", qty: "9" } ] } }

    // §13.16 "Provenance and atomicity": each install carried the root's own
    // change in the same transition, and both landed.
    { watch: "public.entries", id: "we", expect_init: { value: [ { id: "first" }, { id: "second" } ] } }
  ]
}
